# Running heavy jobs on a rented GPU

The user rents a Linux GPU machine for heavy compute. Do not launch long or GPU-heavy
jobs locally (full training runs, multi-seed sweeps, large evaluations). Instead, tell
the user the job is ready and give them the exact commands to run on the rented GPU;
they will run it and paste back the output.

- Fine to run locally: unit/smoke tests, `data.frac=0.02 train.epochs=1` debug runs, quick scripts (< ~5 min).
- Everything else: hand off to the rented GPU.

When handing off, always provide:
1. Whether to `git pull` first (push the changes before handing off).
2. The exact command, e.g. `SEEDS="0 1 2 3 4" MAX_PARALLEL=3 bash scripts/run_seeds.sh configs/phase2_stain.yaml`
   (run inside `tmux`). It trains, then runs `scripts/eval_variants.sh` (last epoch + AdaBN) per seed.
3. Estimated runtime and what to bring back: the `outputs/<experiment>/` folders (or at least the
   `metrics.json`, `eval_*.json` and `preds_*.npz` files) and `logs/`. Then run
   `python scripts/summarize.py` locally to rebuild `results/summary.md`.

Conventions:
- One-time machine setup: `bash scripts/setup_gpu.sh` (dataset into `data/`, override with `DATA_ROOT`).
- Runs resume from `outputs/<experiment>/seed<N>/last.pt`; delete that folder to start a run fresh.
- Experiment configs inherit from `configs/phase1_baseline.yaml` via `base:` and list only what changes.
- Main metric: test (hospital 2) accuracy; always also report sensitivity, specificity, AUROC, ECE. 5 seeds per experiment.
- The repo is public: never commit API keys, SSH keys, tokens, emails, or personal paths.
